From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 17:09:51 +0200
Subject: clk: intel: correct the off-by-one in the CGU field write mask

intel_set_clk_val() builds its mask with GENMASK(width + shift, shift),
which spans width + 1 bits.  Every field write therefore also clears the
bit immediately above the field it was asked to write.

The vendor tree has already fixed this in the same file.  The GPL source
releases for the FRITZ!Box 7590 (07.50 and 08.25) carry a later revision
of drivers/clk/intel/clk-cgu.c -- same filename, same function name,
same signature -- whose helper reads

	u32 mask = GENMASK(width + shift - 1, shift);

That is the form adopted here, verbatim.  This file is shared framework
code rather than per-SoC code: in the 08.25 tree it backs clk-prx300.c,
while the GRX500 support there is a separate legacy CLK_OF_DECLARE
driver.  We drive it from our own clk-grx500.c branch tables instead, so
the helper is common ground between the two trees even though nothing
else in the directory is.  Our copy of it descends from the older 2018
Intel submission that created drivers/clk/intel/ and had the defect from
birth; the 7590 releases carry the same helper after the vendor base it
came from was corrected.

Three further implementations agree.  The reader in this very file is
right (intel_get_clk_val() masks with BIT(width) - 1).  Mainline's fork
is right: drivers/clk/x86/clk-cgu.h, lgm_set_clk_val(), uses
(GENMASK(width - 1, 0) << shift), the same value written the other way
round.  And the older vendor helper is right too: set_clk_val() in
drivers/clk/intel/clk_api.c masks with to_mask(width) = BIT(width) - 1,
byte-identical across every vendor GPL release checked.

One write already goes out with the bad mask on every boot, and hardware
says it is harmless.  CLK_VOICE carries CLOCK_FLAG_VAL_INIT
(clk-grx500.c), so intel_clk_register_fixed() writes IF_CLK bits 15:14 at
every probe with mask 0x1c000 instead of 0xc000, taking CGU IF_CLK
(0x162000c4) bit 16 down with it.  A FRITZ!Box 7590 running the corrected
code still reads 0x00008084 there, exactly as it did before, so bit 16 is
0 out of reset on this silicon and the surplus write was overwriting a
zero with a zero.  Worth stating plainly rather than leaving as a claim of
corruption: this was the only place the defect was reachable today, and
the answer is benign.

The unreachable paths are where it would have mattered.  The eight
pll_div fields sit at shifts 0/4/8/12 with width 4, so the surplus bit
of a set_rate() write lands on bit shift + 4 -- which for four of the
eight is the neighbouring divider's bit 0 (a gswip write would also
clear ddr's LSB, an ssx4 write cpu0's).  The CPU mux is worse: CLK_CPU
is shift 29 width 1 in PLL0A_CFG1, so a set_parent() would clear bit 30,
and bit 30 reads 1 on both boards (PLL0A_CFG1 = 0x60403863 on the
FRITZ!Box 7590).  None of it is reachable without an OPP table or a
cpufreq driver, neither of which exists for intel-mips, but the watchdog
is about to grow a clk rate-change notifier whose whole purpose is to
cope with a CPU-clock change, so the helper has to be correct first.

The three gate call sites pass a hardcoded width of 1 and so write two
bits, but only ever into the write-1-to-act GATE_HW_REG_EN/_DIS registers
with the surplus bit zero, which is inert.  They become exact too.  The
08.25 revision passes the same hardcoded 1 and relies on the same
property.

No caller compensates for the wide mask: every width argument in this
directory comes straight from the intel_clk_branch tables, the macros in
clk-cgu.h apply no arithmetic to it, and both the 08.25 revision and
mainline pass identical widths to their corrected helpers.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/clk/intel/clk-cgu.c b/drivers/clk/intel/clk-cgu.c
index 84dcfa913ee0..f17448377f3f 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/intel/clk-cgu.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/intel/clk-cgu.c
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 void intel_set_clk_val(struct regmap *map, u32 reg, u8 shift,
 		       u8 width, u32 set_val)
 {
-	u32 mask = GENMASK(width + shift, shift);
+	u32 mask = GENMASK(width + shift - 1, shift);
 
 	regmap_update_bits(map, reg, mask, set_val << shift);
 }
